A||N||9||kornholio||torquing wheels||||1
Z||000000||kornholio||10-22-1999||05:04 PM||||Is there a way to torque your wheels without using a torque wrench?  Like, how far from finger tight?  How many of you actually have a torque wrench?  I don't want to spend the money on something I'll use 2 or three times a year.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||10-22-1999||07:54 PM||||I always torque my wheels to specs. Those Fiero brake rotors warp if you even look at them cross-eyed. Buy a torque wrench, I don't know how you can work on a car without one.||||reg||1
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||10-22-1999||10:51 PM||||I paid 85 bucks for my Craftsman torque wrench and torque <B>everything</B> that is supposed to be. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||TOM||10-22-1999||10:55 PM||||I only torque the important stuff. But I have years of experience.||||reg||1
Z||000004||lowCG||10-23-1999||12:03 AM||||I've gotten to the point where I can torque stuff very close(+-5ft lbs)to what it's supposed to be by feel,without the torque wrench,should probably check the book more often;just that every time I do...<BR>I use a breaker-bar/socket for my wheels,about 75ft/lbs,working in a criss-cross,so not to bind,or warp anything.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||10-23-1999||07:03 AM||||I have Craftsman tools too. I had my torque wrench for over 20 years, then last year I broke it. Just went back to Sears and they replaced it for free. Craftsman tools are a little expensive though if you're on a tight budget. I recently saw a torque wrench on sale at O'Reilly's Auto Parts for $20. It looked fairly decent. That might work pretty good for a guy that doesn't use one a lot.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Shiner||10-23-1999||01:44 PM||||I got a nice clicker torque wrench from some traveling sales place.  It IS a name brand, although I forget what brand at the moment.  I paid $20 for it!  We checked to see if it was accurate with my father's good old bar-meter type torque wrench, and it was "Dead on balls accurate"  (you had to see the movie...)  <BR>It was a great deal, and after a little while I started getting a feel for torque, so that I didn't have to use it all the time.  I would DEFINATLY invest in a decent torque wrench if you don't have one, guys!||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||10-23-1999||03:50 PM||||Never torqued a wheel in my life. Lot of other things but never a wheel. Never had any problems even with the Fiero. I've lost count now how many times this year I've had my fiero wheels off for one thing or another.<P>Three things I do tho.... always use a "4 way" wrench. Mine is about 20 inches across. I hold the wrench only by 2 arms when tightening. don't use a cheater to tighten. <P>I Avoid air wrenches to install the wheels. they almost always over tighten things.<P>the last is to anti sieze the threads. Make sure the anti sieze is only used on the threads. this keeps the threads from rusting or stripping, and makes it eisier to hand tighten the wheels. <P>Never allow anti sieze to get on the the lug seats. You Want the cones to bind. That's what locks the lugs and keeps them tight.<P>Should you use a torque wrench? yes, even if I don't. I handle allot of rubber, and I always use the same wrench. If you have any doubt then use the torque wrench. They aren't very expensive, and you'll use it on other things.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fastonpumpgas||10-24-1999||09:17 AM||||wellI figure they are about 100 ound of torque, and I know from useing one inthe shop that , that is abou tas tight as I can get um .  I myself havent ever torque a wheel (flywheel maybe) Does anyone know the torque spec. on a fiero?<P>But a air rachet should never be use, unles install a stud or taking them off.<P>I use a little D-40 and then wrech um back on . ( although my compressor is only good for about 90 ||||reg||12
Z||000009||batboy||10-24-1999||10:33 AM||||Fiero wheel torque specs: steel is 80 ft/lb and aluminum is 100 ft/lbs. Another good practice to minimize rotor warpage is to tighten the lug nuts in a pattern where you skip every other lug nut.||||reg||1
